{# version: 1 #}
Build the notation glossary of a research paper: every mathematical symbol the method uses,
with what it denotes. Use only the text and equations below. Every paragraph starts with an
id in brackets.

For each symbol return:
- "symbol": the symbol as it appears in the equations' LaTeX (e.g. "d_k", "\alpha",
  "W^Q_i", "\mathcal{L}"), or as written in the text if there is no LaTeX.
- "meaning": what it denotes, in a few words ("dimension of the keys").
- "quote": ONE sentence copied character-for-character from the text that defines or
  explains it.
- "chunk_id": the id in brackets of the paragraph the quote comes from.

Include symbols, not words: skip ordinary function names (softmax, max, log) unless the paper
defines them. List each symbol once. If the text does not say what a symbol means, leave it
out rather than guessing.

Equations:
{% for eq in equations -%}
{{ eq.number or "(unnumbered)" }} {{ eq.body }}
{% endfor %}

Text:
{{ text }}
